In terms of its radio and X-ray luminosities, for distances greater than \\sim3 kpc, IGR J17591-2342 would be brighter in the radio than the majority of other accreting NS systems (Figure 2). At a such distances, its L_{\\rm R}/L_{\\rm X} ratio places IGR J17591-2342 in the region of parameter space occupied by BHXRBs. These results imply that IGR J17591-2342 was either considerably radio-loud during the early stages of its 2018 outburst, or it is closer than 3 kpc.

Comparing the observed radio emission from IGR J17591-2342 to the sample of AMXPs with reported radio detections (Figure 2, magenta squares), we see that IGR J17591-2342 appears to be more radio bright than all other NS systems for distances greater than \\sim5 kpc, where this reduces to \\sim3 kpc without the inclusion of the AMXP IGR J17379-3747. The recent radio detection of IGR J17379-3747 (van den Eijnden et al., 2018) indicate this as another potentially radio bright AMXP (Figure 2, magenta star), where an 8 kpc distance upper-limit was estimated from type I X-ray bursts, assuming the peak flux of the bursts reached the empirical NS Eddington limit.

### Absorption along the line of sight

The soft X-ray emission observed from IGR J17591-2342 is highly absorbed. Using ISM abundances from Wilms et al. (2000), we find a best fit from _Swift_-XRT data of N_{\\rm H}=(4.4\\pm0.3)\\times10{}^{22} cm{}^{-2}. This is consistent with results presented by Sanna et al. (2018), where the N_{\\rm H} was determined to be (3.6\\pm1.1)\\times10{}^{22} cm{}^{-2} from _INTEGRAL_, _NuSTAR_, and _Swift_-XRT observations taken at similar times. Our result is marginally higher than that found using the _NICER_ data taken on similar dates (August 15 and August 18), which gave (3.45{}^{+0.18}_{-0.15})\\times10{}^{22} cm{}^{-2} and (3.59{}^{+0.05}_{-0.08})\\times10{}^{22} cm{}^{-2}, respectively (Sanna et al., 2018), agreeing at the \\sim2-\\sigma level. Chandra observations taken on August 23 find N_{\\rm H}=(4.9\\pm0.2)\\times10{}^{22} cm{}^{-2}(Nowak et al., 2018), consistent within the errors of our measured value.

IGR J17591-2342 lies in the direction of the Galactic center (Ducci et al., 2018), with an unknown distance to the source. However, our modelled N_{\\rm H}, which traces the interstellar gas along the line of sight, is \\sim3-4 times higher than the expected Galactic contribution of

